# Check every text file out with LF line endings on EVERY OS.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which turns LF into CRLF on checkout.
# That is not cosmetic here: a large part of the test suite reads SOURCE files and parses
# them — the IPC catalog drift guard (`shared/ipcCatalog.drift.test.ts`), the platform
# contract pins, and the Rust source-text pins over the Kotlin bridge (`test_support.rs`'s
# `kotlin_fn_body` / `rust_production_source`, which cut at `"\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {"`).
# Under CRLF those patterns stop matching and the tests fail on Windows only, for a reason
# that has nothing to do with the code. Prettier's `endOfLine` is also LF, so a CRLF
# checkout fails `npm run format:check` on every file. This attribute overrides autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files must stay CRLF or cmd.exe misparses labels and GOTOs.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Bundled filter lists are data, consumed byte-for-byte; never rewrite them.
src-tauri/resources/** -text

# Binary assets.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
*.jar binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
